        "id": 153518,
        "name": "UXM266",
        "username": "UXM266",
        "avatar_template": "/user_avatar/forums.greaterthangames.com/uxm266/{size}/1966_2.png",
        "created_at": "2016-01-18T19:09:40.000Z",
        "cooked": "<p>My story is ridiculous not because the game itself was crazy, but because the player was just... odd.</p><p>Basically, I brought Sentinels to a gaming event to introduce it to new people, and have some fun. I got there when it opened, so there weren't many people to begin with.&nbsp;</p><p>There was one guy who seemed interested and I explained it to him. I told him the mechanics, the theme, and the important factor \"3-5 players\". Now we were two people. The obvious answer to most veteran players is that \"I can play 2 heroes since I know the characters, he plays 1\". Simple, right? Nope. He refused that. He wanted one hero each. He was so adamant to do it this way, that he wouldn't play even if&nbsp;<strong>I</strong>&nbsp;played two. He honestly thought it would be fine. I explained to him that it will be hard and unbalanced, and that it's not intended for two heroes, but at least three heroes. He wouldn't relent. He assumed we'd be fine.</p><p>So, in a quick 20 minute game (with a set-up made for us to win in most normal circumstances) we lost big time.&nbsp;</p><p>I asked him what he thought. He said something along the lines of \"Way too hard, the game developers don't understand how to balance the game difficulty. They need to learn how to make a game properly.\" All I could answer was \"It is meant for 3-5 players, and I did tell you it will be very hard, and we might not win.\" Still, he was convinced GtG's fault for being bad at what they do…</p><p>We didn't play it again. The other times I introduced again I never had this issue.</p><p>*start sarcasm* So a huuuge thanks goes out to GtG for making a game too unbalanced when it's not played properly, where the recommendations are written on the box, in the rule book, and on your website. Thanks for that! *end sarcasm*</p><p>&nbsp;</p><p>&nbsp;</p>",
        "post_number": 3,
        "post_type": 1,
        "updated_at": "2021-02-07T04:32:52.302Z",
